-The reference submission for `wallet@1`: Python largest-first. It sorts the wallet
-by value, adds inputs until they cover the outputs and the fee (priced with one
-change output), makes a single change output when the leftover is at least min-ada,
-and otherwise folds the leftover into the fee. It refuses only when the whole wallet
-cannot pay. It is the root of the lineage tree and the yardstick for preflight.
+`builds-on baseline`: the same Python largest-first with one change output, plus
+opportunistic consolidation. After largest-first has covered a payment, it also
+spends up to `MAX_EXTRA = 2` of the smallest wallet UTxOs under 2 ADA, skipping any
+worth less than the 6,160-lovelace fee of its own input, and recomputes the change.
+
+Why: on the judge's own scenarios (`contracts/wallet/sim.py`, seeds 1-5), 97% of
+largest-first's final dust is reward-like deposits it never spends, not its own
+change. Directions item 2.
diff --git a/challenges/coin-select/submissions/baseline/select b/challenges/coin-select/submissions/lf-consolidate/select
index dc1f0dd..909ab51 100755
--- a/challenges/coin-select/submissions/baseline/select
